;; Package quic implements the QUIC protocol.
;;
;; This package is a work in progress.
;; It is not ready for production usage.
;; Its API is subject to change without notice.
;;
;; This package is low-level.
;; Most users will use it indirectly through an HTTP/3 implementation.
;;
;; # Usage
;;
;; An [Endpoint] sends and receives traffic on a network address.
;; Create an Endpoint to either accept inbound QUIC connections
;; or create outbound ones.
;;
;; A [Conn] is a QUIC connection.
;;
;; A [Stream] is a QUIC stream, an ordered, reliable byte stream.
;;
;; # Cancellation
;;
;; All blocking operations may be canceled using a context.Context.
;; When performing an operation with a canceled context, the operation
;; will succeed if doing so does not require blocking. For example,
;; reading from a stream will return data when buffered data is available,
;; even if the stream context is canceled.
;;
;; # Limitations
;;
;; This package is a work in progress.
;; Known limitations include:
;;
;;   - Performance is untuned.
;;   - 0-RTT is not supported.
;;   - Address migration is not supported.
;;   - Server preferred addresses are not supported.
;;   - The latency spin bit is not supported.
;;   - Stream send/receive windows are configurable,
;;     but are fixed and do not adapt to available throughput.
;;   - Path MTU discovery is not implemented.
;;
;; # Security Policy
;;
;; This package is a work in progress,
;; and not yet covered by the Go security policy.
(in-ns 'go.vendor.golang_org.x.net.quic) (go/file "doc.go" :doc "Package quic implements the QUIC protocol.\n\nThis package is a work in progress.\nIt is not ready for production usage.\nIts API is subject to change without notice.\n\nThis package is low-level.\nMost users will use it indirectly through an HTTP/3 implementation.\n\n# Usage\n\nAn [Endpoint] sends and receives traffic on a network address.\nCreate an Endpoint to either accept inbound QUIC connections\nor create outbound ones.\n\nA [Conn] is a QUIC connection.\n\nA [Stream] is a QUIC stream, an ordered, reliable byte stream.\n\n# Cancellation\n\nAll blocking operations may be canceled using a context.Context.\nWhen performing an operation with a canceled context, the operation\nwill succeed if doing so does not require blocking. For example,\nreading from a stream will return data when buffered data is available,\neven if the stream context is canceled.\n\n# Limitations\n\nThis package is a work in progress.\nKnown limitations include:\n\n  - Performance is untuned.\n  - 0-RTT is not supported.\n  - Address migration is not supported.\n  - Server preferred addresses are not supported.\n  - The latency spin bit is not supported.\n  - Stream send/receive windows are configurable,\n    but are fixed and do not adapt to available throughput.\n  - Path MTU discovery is not implemented.\n\n# Security Policy\n\nThis package is a work in progress,\nand not yet covered by the Go security policy.\n" :imports [])
